    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Code and patterns",
      "question": "Write Kahn's algorithm, and name problems it solves.",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. Write Kahn's algorithm from memory, and name the problems it solves. Pause and sketch it out.",
          "pauseAfter": 4,
          "props": {
            "question": "Write Kahn's algorithm, and <em>name its problems</em>."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "say": "Compute every in-degree first. Seed the queue with all zero in-degree vertices. Pop one, append it to the order, and decrement each neighbor. A neighbor that reaches zero joins the queue. Finally, if the order is shorter than n, report a cycle.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Kahn's algorithm, <em>in code</em>",
            "file": "topo_sort.cpp",
            "tag": "O(V + E)",
            "size": 21,
            "width": 1060,
            "code": "vector<int> topoSort(int n, const vector<vector<int>>& adj) {\n    vector<int> indegree(n, 0);\n    for (int u = 0; u < n; ++u)\n        for (int v : adj[u]) indegree[v]++;\n    queue<int> q;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n        if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);\n    vector<int> order;\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        int u = q.front(); q.pop();\n        order.push_back(u);\n        for (int v : adj[u])\n            if (--indegree[v] == 0) q.push(v);\n    }\n    if ((int)order.size() != n) return {};  // cycle\n    return order;\n}",
            "highlights": [
              {
                "at": "compute",
                "lines": "2-4",
                "note": "Count incoming edges"
              },
              {
                "at": "seed",
                "lines": "5-7",
                "note": "Start from every in-degree-0 vertex"
              },
              {
                "at": "append",
                "lines": "10-11",
                "note": "Pop and output"
              },
              {
                "at": "decrement",
                "lines": "12-13",
                "note": "Decrement; enqueue at zero"
              },
              {
                "at": "finally",
                "lines": "15",
                "note": "Short output → cycle"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "It shows up everywhere. Course schedules and build orders are dependency resolution. Processing the queue level by level gives parallel scheduling, and the number of levels is the minimum time. Swap the queue for a min-heap to get the lexicographically smallest order. And in a DAG, relaxing edges in topological order gives longest or shortest paths in linear time.",
